Cactus Code Thorn TargetTracker
Thorn Author(s)     : Jordan Nicoules
                      Miguel Zilhão
Thorn Maintainer(s) : Jordan Nicoules
                      Miguel Zilhão
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Purpose of the thorn:

Track the locations of a number of targets during an evolution and update the
locations of associated `SphericalSurface`s.

It can be used together with any thorn computing suitable grid scalars, and the
grid scalars computed by TargetTracker itself (`target_loc_*`, `target_com_*`)
can be fed back into it or into other thorns.

The targets are configured with per-target parameters (10 targets by
default, see `nmax_targets`), in the style of `SphericalSurface`.

It is inspired by various thorns from the Einstein Toolkit and outside it,
including `UAv_Analysis`, `NSTracker`, and `VolumeIntegrals_GRMHD`.


Tracking targets
----------------

### Basic usage

For each target i (0 <= i < nmax_targets), set `track[i] = yes` and
provide the grid scalars to track with `target_x/y/z[i]`, e.g.

    TargetTracker::track               [0] = yes
    TargetTracker::target_x            [0] = "PunctureTracker::pt_loc_x[0]"
    TargetTracker::target_y            [0] = "PunctureTracker::pt_loc_y[0]"
    TargetTracker::target_z            [0] = "PunctureTracker::pt_loc_z[0]"
    TargetTracker::which_surface_to_store_info[0] = 0

The tracked coordinates are stored and updated in the public grid scalars
`TargetTracker::target_loc_x/y/z[i]` at every tracking iteration
(see `track_every`, `start_tracking_after_time`, `stop_tracking_after_time`).
Mid-run (de)activation is done through the `track` parameter, not these
complementary parameters (they can be steered at recovery).
If `which_surface_to_store_info[i]` is set, the centroid of the given
spherical surface is set to the tracked location, and the surface is
marked as active/valid. This, in particular, moves `SphericalSurface`s
(and thus e.g. `AHFinderDirect`'s or `QuasiLocalMeasures`'s surfaces)
during the evolution, as long as the corresponding grid scalars are
updated. (The user must ensure the surface resolutions `ntheta`/`nphi`
etc. are set.)

A target with `target_*` set to "" is "fixed": it does not move
(its location is set by `initial_x/y/z[i]` or the latest computed location).

### Surface-following mode ('surface to tracker')

Setting `loc_from_surface_mode[i] = yes` inverts the data flow: the
tracker does not read grid scalars but instead copies the centroid of
its associated surface into `target_loc`. This is useful to carry a
tracker across the formation of a new horizon: once `AHFinderDirect`
finds the horizon and stores it into the surface, the tracker keeps
the location, continuously, from the surface.

### Tracking the opposite, and ad hoc adjustments

Two methods allow to derive the tracked location from the tracked
scalar(s), for cases not covered by a suitable grid scalar (e.g. when
tracking the mirror of a puncture in a symmetry run):

  - `track_opposite[i] = yes`: track the opposite of the target
    (loc = -target). This takes precedence over the next method.

  - `tracker_adjust[i] = yes` with `tracker_x/y/z_adjust_factor[i]`
    and `tracker_x/y/z_adjust_origin[i]`: apply an affine adjustment
    loc = factor * target + origin, for each direction independently.
    A fixed dimension (empty target name) is not adjusted.


Self-contained center of mass
-----------------------------

For each target with `compute_com[i] = yes`, TargetTracker computes the
center of mass of the matter distribution,

    CoM^i = \int x^i rho alpha sqrt(gamma) dV / \int rho alpha sqrt(gamma) dV,

with rho = n^mu n^nu T_mu_nu built from the stress-energy tensor of
`TmunuBase`. It is scheduled to run *before* the tracking itself, so that the
results can be tracked in the same pass. The results are stored in the public,
checkpointed grid scalars

    TargetTracker::target_com_x/y/z[i]   (center of mass)
    TargetTracker::target_com_mass[i]    (mass integral, i.e. the denominator)

and can be output, tracked (with the parameters `target_*[i]`, by this
thorn or any other), or used to move the corresponding puncture/surface.
The center of mass computation is done independently of the status of the tracker.

Restricting the integral to a spherical domain centered at the tracked
point is done with `com_compute_inside_radius[i]` and `com_compute_outside_radius[i]`
(negative = no restriction):

    com_compute_outside_radius[i] < r <= com_compute_inside_radius[i]

with r the coordinate distance to `target_loc_*[i]`. The (inclusive
inside, exclusive outside) conventions are complementary, so that
inside + outside integrals add up to the global one. This is useful
in particular for tracking local lumps of matter independently (e.g.
in binary systems), and the sphere moves with the tracker.

If the total center of mass is requested more than once at a given iteration,
the values are recycled from the first computation.

The integrands are computed on all refinement levels (a single
set of grid functions is recomputed and reduced sequentially for each
target, in the spirit of `VolumeIntegrals_GRMHD`), and reduced with the
driver's sum reduction, which handles mesh refinement. The following
global parameters control the calculation:

  - `com_every`: do the calculation every N iterations (for each tracker
    independently). Like for `track_every`, mid-run (de)activation is
    controlled by the `compute_com` parameter. Note that the
    calculation requires the stress-energy tensor of the current pass
    (the user must make sure `TmunuBase` storage is on and the tensor
    is up to date, e.g. with `TmunuBase::stress_energy_at_RHS = yes`).

  - `excise_horizon`: exclude the region inside the (apparent) horizons
    from the integration. This relies on the mask set by `AHFinderDirect`
    (SpaceMask type "mask", state "outside").

  - `com_symmetry`: multiply the integrands by the appropriate factors
    for runs with symmetries ("none", "bitant_x/y/z", ...). 
    "none" can be used even when the grid has symmetries, but it is 
    then up to the user to tackle the symmetry factors.

If the mass integral vanishes (e.g. an empty sphere), the previous
values are kept and a warning printed (the calculation becomes trivially
ill-defined).

Since the integrands are private to the thorn, the grid functions do not
need to be sync'ed between calculations for correctness of the reduction;
they are nevertheless synced to make their (ghost) values meaningful for
output. The reduction follows the driver's weights (in particular,
half-weighting grid layers on symmetry planes, as required to normalize
symmetric integrals).

Steerability
------------

The following control parameters are STEERABLE=ALWAYS and can be steered
at runtime (e.g. with the `Trigger` thorn):

  - `track[i]`: whether to track target i
  - `target_x/y/z[i]`: the grid scalars to track for target i (empty = fixed)
  - `loc_from_surface_mode[i]`: whether to copy the centroid of the associated surface into the tracker
  - `track_opposite[i]`: whether to track the opposite of the target
  - `tracker_adjust[i]`: whether to apply an affine adjustment to the tracked location
  - `compute_com[i]`: whether to compute the center of mass for target i
  - `verbose`: whether to print information about the tracking and CoM computation

Internal flags are set accordingly, so that their values can be recovered
from checkpoints. If the recovered value conflicts with the parameter file, the internal value is used and a warning is printed.
Use `force_params_at_recovery[i] = yes` to override this behavior for target i.

Other parameters are STEERABLE=RECOVER and taken from the parameter file at recovery.
See `param.ccl` and `src/Initialize.c` for details.

> WARNING: It seems that commenting a parameter at recovery will keep the value from the checkpoint (e.g. for stop_tracking_after_time).
> This is Cactus behavior, not this thorn.

Schedule placement
------------------

The thorn runs at ANALYSIS so that it can run after `Trigger_Check`.
The center of mass group is scheduled as `global-early`, and therefore runs
before the tracking: the grid scalars it produces can be tracked in the same
pass (by this thorn or others). Note the one-pass lag for a *self-tracking*
restriction window (the sphere is centered on the previous pass's tracked
point): same feedback design as `VolumeIntegrals_GRMHD`'s moving spheres.

WARNING: `after AHFinderDirect_maybe_do_masks` is not part of this schedule:
with `excise_horizon = yes`, the user must make sure the mask is up to date at
ANALYSIS (e.g. run `AHFinderDirect` at CCTK_POSTSTEP, as in its default
configuration, or at ANALYSIS before this thorn — see the discussion in the
schedule.ccl of both thorns). In particular, `AHFinderDirect_maybe_do_masks` is
not scheduled `global-early`, so it does not necessarily run before this group
at ANALYSIS.

Other schedule locations: `ParamCheck` (parameter checks), `INITIAL`
and `CCTK_POST_RECOVER_VARIABLES` (initialization, recovery),
`CCTK_INITIAL`/`CCTK_POST_RECOVER_VARIABLES` (counter safety),
`BASEGRID` (flags for the CoM: multipatch detection, symmetry factors).

**TODO/IN PROGRESS:**
Management of `sf_active` and `sf_valid` flags, and possible modifications
to the schedule placement because of it.
See the discussion in `TODO.md` for details.


Tests
-----

A small testsuite is provided in `test/` (run with e.g.
`make <config>-testsuite`). It uses standard ET thorns only (plus the matter
scalar field of `Scalar/Scalar*`, evolved with `ScalarEvolve` on a static exact
flat metric), so that the expected values are known analytically:

  - `track_adjust_opposite`: affine adjustment (`tracker_adjust`) and
    tracking the opposite (`track_opposite`) of a moving target.
  - `track_surface_roundtrip`: a surface handover, as when a horizon forms: a
    tracker following a grid scalar writes a surface, then a second tracker is
    activated and takes the surface over, while the first one switches to
    'loc_from_surface_mode' and follows it. The new owner is then moved, and
    the first tracker has to follow it.
  - `track_timing`: `start_tracking_after_time`, `stop_tracking_after_time`,
    `track_every > 1`, steering `track` on and off, steering `target_*` to
    another grid scalar and to "" (fixed), and (at the end of the run)
    steering `target_*` to an invalid name, which deactivates the target and
    terminates the run by design (the reference output ends there).
  - `com_restrict_identity`: the volume-restriction identities
    `inside + outside == global` and
    `inside(r1) + shell(r1,r2] == inside(r2)`, checked to machine precision by
    the `util/check_restriction` postprocessor; also exercises `com_every > 1`.
  - `com_empty_guard`: a region that contains matter and is then moved to an
    empty region; the code keeps the previously computed values (not the
    initial ones) and warns (no NaN/inf). A mirror tracker follows the frozen
    CoM and keeps computing its own.
  - `com_self_tracking`: a tracker that follows the (restricted) center of
    mass it computes; the window converges over a few passes, exercising the
    feedback loop and the one-pass lag.
  - `com_symmetry_bitant`: a constant scalar on a bitant `z >= 0` box with
    `com_symmetry = "bitant_z"`; the resulting mass and center of mass agree
    with the full-box `com_symmetry = "none"` values.
  - `com_recover_tracker`: recovers from a committed checkpoint and checks
    the continuation of the trackers and of `force_params_at_recovery`.

`Trigger` is used to drive the tests: it can steer `target_*` (to another
grid-scalar name, to "", or to an invalid name), `track`,
`loc_from_surface_mode`, `compute_com`, and (through `steerscalar`) write
literal values into grid scalars. Since a target only moves because its source
does, the tests use a few untracked "value carrier" targets whose
`target_loc_*` is written by `Trigger`, and which the moving targets follow;
this keeps the tests lightweight but the motion is synthetic. A fully
dynamical setting (e.g. tracking punctures, which needs BSSN and actual
punctures) would be much heavier and is not part of the unit tests.

Note: `target_*` is validated as a grid-scalar *name* (or ""), so a literal
value cannot be steered into it; that is why the `steerscalar` trick is used
to make a target move.

Note that the CoM calculation lags the tracking by one pass: the CoM group
runs before `TargetTracker_SetSurfaces`, so it uses the `target_loc` from the
previous pass. In particular, after a target is moved by a trigger, the CoM
restriction window only follows it on the next iteration (and the matter field
itself evolves, so the masses also change slightly from pass to pass).

Reference output is `CarpetIOASCII` 0D data, compared with a relative tolerance
of 1e-10 (comment/header lines are ignored by the test framework).

Not covered by the testsuite:

  - `excise_horizon`: it needs a `SpaceMask` "mask"/"outside" mask. Among the
    standard ET thorns, only `AHFinderDirect` registers that mask type/state
    (`GRHydro` only reads/writes its own excision mask), so a cheap test would
    require either a helper thorn or a BH initial-data/evolution configuration
    (e.g. a wave on a Kerr-Schild background from the Gallery). It was verified
    manually against `UAv_Analysis` in excised HairyBH runs.


Known limitations
-----------------

  - 10 targets maximum (crutch parameter `nmax_targets`).

  - The thorn is scheduled at ANALYSIS only (no POSTSTEP option as of
    yet; see schedule.ccl and TODO.md).

  - The CoM restriction is a sphere centered at the tracked point; the
    radius is a fixed constant parameter (in particular it does not follow a
    possibly dynamic/non-spherical surface).
    Arbitrary integrands are not provided. If this is a wish for
    future development, it would be worth considering outsourcing it to
    a separate thorn (see for instance `VolumeIntegrals_GRMHD`).

  - Only Carpet(-style) drivers are supported/relevant (in particular
    for the reduction weights), and no accelerator offloading is done.

  - Checkpoint recovery of parameters: STEERABLE=ALWAYS parameters are
    recovered through checkpointed internal flags (see the recovery
    section above and the caveats therein). STEERABLE=RECOVER parameters
    are taken from the parameter file. The tracker grid scalars
    (`target_loc`, `target_com`) are checkpointed as well.

  - Checkpoint recovery and `Carpet::max_timelevels`: with the default
    `Carpet::prolongation_order_time = 1`, the automatic
    `Carpet::max_timelevels = prolongation_order_time+1 = 2` is smaller than
    the 3 time levels requested, e.g., by `ADMBase` and `ScalarBase`.
    Evolution still runs, but restarting such a run with `IO::recover`
    aborts in `CarpetIOHDF5::Recover` (a `CarpetLib` timelevel assertion)
    while synchronising the recovered variables. Setting
    `Carpet::max_timelevels` (or `prolongation_order_time`) explicitly avoids
    this; for example `Carpet::max_timelevels = 3` (or
    `Carpet::prolongation_order_time = 2`, as in most BH parameter files)
    makes recovery work. This is a driver/configuration issue, not a
    TargetTracker one, and it is reproducible with the plain
    `Scalar/ScalarEvolve/test/SF_constantID_test.par` parameter file (no
    TargetTracker active). The `com_recover_tracker` test sets
    `Carpet::max_timelevels` explicitly for this reason.
